//BUG="a tab inside a string literal is turned into four spaces"
//OUT="a\tb"

// The string below holds a real tab character between `a` and `b`.
//
// `parse_strings_and_comments` (src/compiler/preprocessor.rs) starts by
// running `tabs_to_spaces` over the whole file, before it has worked out what
// is a string and what is code. Expanding tabs is right for indentation
// (00_lexical/12_tabs_count_as_four_spaces.cpl) and wrong inside quotes: the
// program prints `a    b`, four spaces, and the tab the source asked for is
// gone without a diagnostic.
//
// A character literal holding a tab does not survive either — it becomes
// four spaces between the quotes and is rejected with "Expected quote". And
// since `\t` is not a recognised escape (00_lexical/65e), `char 9` is the
// only way left to produce a tab at all, which matters for any problem whose
// output is tab-separated.
//
// The fix is to expand tabs only where indentation is measured, in
// `parse_indentation`, and leave the characters of the file alone.

fn main
    out "a	b"
